  'char.to-str': `
converts to the UTF-8 encoded string
s: a single-character UTF-8 string built from that code point
`
  'char.to-txt': `
converts to a UTF-8 encoded txt (the fixed 256-byte structure)
Reuses char.to-bytes to obtain the 1 to 4 byte UTF-8 sequence, then copies it into the txt buffer byte by byte.
Consistent with str.to-txt / []byte.to-txt it returns a plain txt (converting a single code point cannot fail, so no ?txt).
With it, txt.to-upper / to-lower and friends can be rewritten as "take a char -> char.to-lower -> char.to-txt",
no longer depending on indexed writes through txt[i], which returns a char.
out: txt loaded with the code point's UTF-8 sequence (remaining bytes zero-filled)
`
  'char.to-upper': `
converts to upper case (ASCII only)
result: the character converted lower→upper (non a-z returned unchanged)
`
  'chars.to-str': `
⚠️ Note: as of 2026-09-06 the nolang compiler's support for the []char slice type is incomplete; this method is not yet usable:
- legacy backend: []char with-cap/push mishandles the len/cap units (element count vs byte count),
makes .len() report too many elements, and []char.to-str then reads out of bounds and produces garbage (measured: a single push(72)
also outputs "H"+garbage). []char literals and index reads themselves have correct i32 stride, but what push constructs
the slice len field is corrupted.
- MIR=3 gate: the compiler supports neither the []char element type nor the new []t.to-chars generic method,
directly reports "unknown callee []t.to-chars" and cannot compile.
Do not rely on to-chars / []char.to-str until the compiler fixes []char slice support.
`
