  'global.eprint': `
prints the string to stderr with a newline, supporting {name:spec} named format fields
eprint('error: {msg}')
Multiple arguments: eprint('err:', a, b) separated by spaces, with exactly one newline at line end;
Each string literal is its own template (same as print)
s str: format template string (same rules as print); any number of named actual arguments may follow, filled in order
`
  'global.exit': `
terminates the run (exits the current process with status code c)
exit(0)   -> normal exit
exit(1)   → abnormal exit
c i64: process exit code (0 = success, non-zero = failure by convention; values outside 0–255 are truncated by the OS)
`
  'global.format': `
Formats a string using {name:spec} named fields and returns the result (replacing sprintf)
s = format('{x:.2f}')          -> '3.14'
s = format('{name} is {age}')  -> 'Alice is 30'
⚠️ Only a single format string is accepted (there is no multi-argument form)
s str: format template string (same rules as print), and it must be the only argument
out str: the new string after template expansion (not written to any stream)
`
  'global.print': `
prints the string to stdout with a newline, supporting {name:spec} named format fields
print('hello {name}')       -> hello world (when name = 'world')
print('{x:.2f}')            -> 3.14 (when x = 3.14159)
Multiple arguments: print(a, b, c) separated by spaces, with exactly one newline at line end.
With multiple arguments each string literal is its own template (see "with multiple arguments, literals default to templates" above):
print('result={val}', 42, 'result={val}')  -> result=42 42 result=42
s str: format template string; {name:spec} takes the value by matching literal name, and any number of named actual arguments may follow, filled in order
`
  'global.with-cap': `
Create a string or slice with the given capacity (len=0); the type is inferred from the left side of the assignment
s str = with-cap(256)       → a str preallocated with 256 bytes
v []i64 = with-cap(100)     → a slice preallocated with 100 elements
cap i64: the capacity to pre-allocate (bytes for str, element count for vec); the length stays 0
`
  'global.with-cap-len': `
Create a string or slice with the given capacity and length; the type is inferred from the left side of the assignment
v []i64 = with-cap-len(200, 100)  → a slice with capacity 200 and length 100
cap i64: pre-allocated capacity (may exceed len, leaving room for later pushes)
len i64: the actual length; index and read/write directly without pushing first
`
  'global.with-len': `
Create a string or slice with the given length; the type is inferred from the left side of the assignment
s str = with-len(10)        → a str of length 10
v []i64 = with-len(100)     → a slice of length 100
len i64: target length (bytes for str, element count for vec); len=cap allocates once so elements can be written by index without push
`
  'gzip.bit-reader.read-bit': `
Read one bit
Returns 0 when out of range, so the DEFLATE decoder does not crash on over-reads
bit i64: the bit read (0 or 1); after bytes are exhausted it always returns 0 without an error
`
  'gzip.bit-reader.read-bits': `
Read n bits (LSB-first)
n i64: number of bits to read consecutively
v i64: the assembled integer; bits read first go into the low positions (the i-th bit lands at bit i)
`
  'gzip.bit-reader.read-u16-le': `
Read a 2-byte little-endian integer (when byte-aligned)
Takes values directly from data[pos] and data[pos+1] (bypassing the bit buffer) and advances pos by 2.
v i64: the decoded integer in 0..65535
`
  'gzip.bit-reader.skip-to-byte': 'Skip to the next byte boundary'
  'gzip.build-dynamic': `
Build the dynamic Huffman tables from the bit stream
First reads HLIT/HDIST/HCLEN, then the code-length codes in code-order, finally expands repeat codes into the literal/distance tables.
br bit-reader: bit source (the read position advances)
lc huffman: output parameter, filled with the literal/length table
dc huffman: output parameter, filled with the distance table
`
  'gzip.build-fixed': `
Build the fixed Huffman tables
Fills the fixed code lengths per DEFLATE spec §3.2.6: 0-143 length 8, 144-255 length 9,
256-279 length 7, 280-287 length 8; distance codes are all 5 bits.
lc huffman: output parameter, filled with the 288-symbol literal/length table
dc huffman: output parameter, filled with the 30-symbol distance table
`
  'gzip.deflate-stored': `
DEFLATE compression using stored blocks
data: raw data ([]byte)
out: compressed DEFLATE data ([]byte)
`
  'gzip.gzip-compress': `
Compress data using the gzip format
data: raw data ([]byte)
out: gzip-compressed data ([]byte)
`
  'gzip.gzip-decompress': `
Decompress data in gzip format
data: gzip compressed data ([]byte)
out: decompressed data ([]byte)
`
  'gzip.huffman.build': `
Build a Huffman code table from a code-length array
Uses direct indexed assignment to fixed array fields, avoiding dangling pointers caused by local vec assignment
lengths []i64: code length of each symbol, 0 means the symbol is unused
n i64: number of valid symbols (288 for the literal/length table, 30 for the distance table); entries beyond n are never read
`
  'gzip.huffman.decode': `
Decode one symbol
Extends the canonical code bit by bit, locating it via a binary-style search over count[]; returns -1 when no hit after length 15.
br bit-reader: bit source; takes 1 bit per round
sym i64: decoded symbol value (literal/length or distance), -1 = illegal code
`
  'gzip.inflate-decompress': `
Decompress raw DEFLATE data (RFC 1951, ZIP method 8)
data: compressed data ([]byte)
out-size: expected decompressed size (i64)
out: decompressed data ([]byte)
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.add': `
Adds an element; returns whether it was inserted
linear probing finds an empty slot; re-adding the same key does not change the set, it just returns false.
with the table full and the key absent, probing loops forever; the caller must keep the load factor under control.
key i64: the element to add
is-new bool: true=it was absent and got inserted, false=already existed, unchanged
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.clear': 'Clears the set'
  'hash-set.hash-set.contains': `
Reports whether an element exists
key i64: the element to query
found bool: true=the element exists in the set, false=absent (returns false immediately when the set is empty)
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.for-each': `
Iterates over every element
Walks [0, cap) in slot-index order (not insertion order), assigning each occupied slot's key to val in turn.
This language has no callbacks; when the function returns, val stops at the element of the last non-empty slot;
to process items one by one, use an index loop with contains / .keys access instead.
val i64: the element value of the current slot (the last assignment)
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.hash': `
Computes the slot index
key i64: the key to hash (this set only holds i64)
idx i64: key's initial slot within [0, cap) (key & (cap-1), cap must be a power of two)
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.init': 'Initialises the set'
  'hash-set.hash-set.is-empty': `
Whether the set is empty
empty bool: true=size == 0, false=elements remain
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n i64: current number of elements present in the set (adding a new element +1, removing successfully −1)
`
  'hash-set.hash-set.remove': `
Removes an element; returns whether it was removed
Only clears the occupancy flag of that slot without rearranging the probe chain afterward; adding the same key again reuses the slot.
key i64: the element to delete
removed bool: true=found and deleted, false=absent
`
  'heap.empty': `
reports whether the heap is empty
yes bool: true means n == 0 (a push ignored due to fullness does not change this)
`
  'heap.init': `
factory — builds an empty heap on a caller-supplied element buffer
data []t: the element buffer; its length sets the heap capacity limit (n is reset to 0, buffer contents not cleared)
h heap: the initialized heap instance, cap = len(data), n = 0
`
  'heap.peek': `
looks at the smallest element without removing it
val ?t: ok=the current heap top (minimum), nil=the heap is empty
`
  'heap.pop': `
pops the smallest element and sifts down
val ?t: ok=the current heap top (minimum), nil=the heap is empty
`
  'heap.push': `
pushes an element and restores the min-heap property
val t: the element to push, type t determined by the buffer type of heap.init
When the heap is full (n >= cap) this push is simply ignored, with no error reported
`
  'heap.size': `
returns the number of elements currently stored in the heap
sz i64: number of elements pushed but not yet popped (0..cap)
`
  'hkdf.hkdf-derive-secret': `
Derive-Secret(Secret, Label, Messages) (TLS 1.3 §7.1)
Equivalent to hkdf-expand-label-vec(secret, label, messages, 32).
secret []byte: 32-byte key
label str: label (without the "tls13 " prefix)
messages []byte: handshake message hash (SHA-256 output, used as the context)
out []byte: 32-byte derived key
`
  'hkdf.hkdf-expand': `
HKDF-Expand (RFC 5869 §2.3)
prk [32]byte: pseudo-random key
info []byte: context info (may be empty; fed into every HMAC round)
out-len i64: required output length; RFC 5869 caps it at 255*32 = 8160 — beyond that the counter wraps and the result no longer conforms to the spec
returns okm []byte: expanded key material (exactly out-len bytes)
`
  'hkdf.hkdf-expand-hmac': `
Compute HMAC-SHA256(prk, t-prefix || info || counter-byte)
Internal helper to avoid mutating slice.len() inside hkdf-expand
prk-vec []byte: key (32 bytes)
t-data []byte: T(i) prefix (may be empty, effective length given by t-len)
t-len i64: effective length of T(i)
info []byte: context info (may be empty)
counter i64: counter byte (RFC requires 1-255); 0 means omit this byte
returns out [32]byte: result of HMAC-SHA256(prk, T(i-1) || info || counter)
`
  'hkdf.hkdf-expand-label': `
TLS 1.3 HKDF-Expand-Label (RFC 8446 §7.1)
Assembles the info per the HkdfLabel structure (length + "tls13 " + label + context) first, then calls HKDF-Expand.
prk [32]byte: pseudo-random key (32 bytes, usually from HKDF-Extract)
label str: label (without the "tls13 " prefix; this function adds it automatically)
context []byte: context (usually the handshake message hash; may be empty)
out-len i64: number of output bytes required
okm []byte: expanded key material, exactly out-len bytes long
`
  'hkdf.hkdf-expand-label-vec': `
Same as hkdf-expand-label but prk arrives as []byte (shorter inputs are zero-padded to 32 bytes)
prk []byte: pseudo-random key, the first 32 bytes are used
label str: label (without the "tls13 " prefix)
context []byte: context (may be empty)
out-len i64: number of output bytes required
okm []byte: expanded key material, exactly out-len bytes long
`
  'hkdf.hkdf-extract': `
HKDF-Extract (RFC 5869 §2.2)
salt []byte: salt (may be empty; 32 zero bytes are substituted when empty)
ikm []byte: input keying material (any length)
returns prk [32]byte: the pseudo-random key computed as HMAC-SHA256(salt, IKM)
`
  'hmac.hmac-md5': `
Compute HMAC-MD5 (MD5 as the underlying hash, block size 64)
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 64-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out [16]byte: 16-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-md5-vec': `
Compute HMAC-MD5, returning []byte
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 64-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out []byte: 16-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha1': `
Compute HMAC-SHA1
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 64-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out [20]byte: 20-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha1-vec': `
Compute HMAC-SHA1, returning []byte
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 64-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out []byte: 20-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha256': `
Compute HMAC-SHA256
key: hashing key (keys longer than the underlying hash block size of 64 bytes are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out [32]byte: 32-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha256-vec': `
Compute HMAC-SHA256, returning []byte (convenient for chaining)
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 64-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out []byte: 32-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha384': `
Compute HMAC-SHA384 (SHA-384 as the underlying hash, block size 128)
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 128-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out [48]byte: 48-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha384-vec': `
Compute HMAC-SHA384, returning []byte
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 128-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out []byte: 48-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha512': `
Compute HMAC-SHA512
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 128-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out [64]byte: 64-byte HMAC value
`
  'hmac.hmac-sha512-vec': `
Compute HMAC-SHA512, returning []byte
key: hashing key (keys longer than the 128-byte block are first hashed down; shorter keys are zero-padded)
msg: message bytes to authenticate
returns out []byte: 64-byte HMAC value
`
  'hpack.decode-int': `
Decode a variable-length integer from data at pos
data str: HPACK-encoded data
pos i64: byte offset of the first byte
prefix-bits i64: number of prefix bits (must match the encoder side)
val i64: decoded integer value (not guaranteed on error)
next-pos i64: offset of the next unread byte (-1=out of range or continuation chain too long)
`
  'hpack.decode-str': `
Decode a string from data at pos
data str: HPACK-encoded data
pos i64: offset of the first byte of the length prefix
out str: decoded source string (Huffman encoding is not supported)
next-pos i64: offset of the next unread byte (-1=error or insufficient data)
`
  'hpack.encode-int': `
Encode a variable-length integer into out
val i64: non-negative integer to encode
prefix-bits i64: number of usable prefix bits in the first byte (1-8); values below 2^prefix-bits-1 fit in a single byte
prefix-byte i64: prefix byte with the high bits already filled (the top 8-prefix-bits bits are set, the low bits are left for the prefix value)
out str: encoding result (first byte plus continuation bytes; each byte carries 7 value bits with the top bit as the continuation flag)
`
  'hpack.encode-str': `
Encode a string (literal, H = 0)
s str: string to encode (varint length prefix + UTF-8 bytes)
out str: encoded byte string (7-bit prefix length encoding concatenated with the raw text; Huffman is not supported)
`
  'hpack.tables.decode-headers': `
Decode a header block
data str: HPACK-encoded header block binary data
names [128]str: decoded header field names, index-aligned with values (unused slots are empty strings)
values [128]str: decoded header field values
count i64: number of headers actually decoded (truncated beyond 128 entries)
ok bool: true=the whole block decoded successfully, false=an error was hit mid-way or the output slots ran out
`
  'hpack.tables.dyn-add': `
Add a header to the dynamic table (FIFO, new entries at index 0)
name: header field name to insert (e.g. :method)
value: the header field's value
`
  'hpack.tables.encode-header': `
Encode a single header
Strategy: exact match → indexed; name match → literal with indexing (using the name index);
otherwise → literal with indexing (new name)
name str: header field name to encode (indexed or literal form is chosen by table lookup)
value str: header field value to encode
out str: encoded header byte string; the encoding result is also added to the dynamic table
`
  'hpack.tables.find': `
Exact match (name + value), returning the global index (static 1-61, dynamic 62+)
returns idx = 0 means not found, idx > 0 = the index
name: header field name to look up
value: header field value to look up (must match name exactly as well)
idx: the matched global index (0 means not found)
`
  'hpack.tables.find-name': `
Match the name only, returning the global index
returns idx = 0 means not found, idx > 0 = the index
name: header field name to look up
idx: index of the name-matched entry (0 means not found)
`
  'hpack.tables.init': 'Initialize the HPACK tables (static table + dynamic table)'
  'hpack.tables.lookup': `
Get name/value by index
idx 1-61 = static table, 62+ = dynamic table
returns name, value, ok = true when found
idx: global index to look up (1-61 queries the static table, 62+ the dynamic table)
name: the header field name found
value: the header field value found
ok: true when idx falls in a valid range and the lookup succeeded
`
  'http.delete': `
Perform an HTTP DELETE request
url: full URL
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.do': `
Perform an HTTP request
method: HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.)
url: full URL (e.g. http://host:port/path)
body: request body (GET should pass an empty string)
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.do-req': `
Send a request using request (supports custom headers)
req: initialized request (with custom headers)
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.download': `
Streaming download (supports any size, written directly to a file)
Uses native TLS/net, no dependence on external tools (curl etc.).
url str: full URL (http:// uses plaintext, https:// uses native TLS, any other scheme fails immediately)
dest str: destination file path; the response body is written on success, and an existing file is deleted on failure
ok bool: true=a response was obtained with a 2xx HTTP status code and the body has been written to dest
`
  'http.get': `
Perform an HTTP GET request
url: full URL
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.get-auth': `
Perform an HTTP GET request with Basic authentication
url str: full URL
user str: user name
password str: password
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.http-dl-dechunk': `
De-frame a chunked-encoded body into raw bytes
b: chunked-encoded response body (each chunk is a hex length line + data + CRLF, terminated by a zero-length chunk)
out: raw bytes with the chunk framing removed (returns the part decoded so far early when a length line is illegal)
`
  'http.http-dl-location': `
Find the Location value in header text (case-insensitive)
head-str: response header text (all header lines)
loc: ok = the Location value, nil = header not present
`
  'http.http-dl-open': `
The actual download (recurses through 3xx redirects; depth counts hops already taken)
url: the full URL currently requested
dest: destination file path (streamed, supports any size)
depth: number of 3xx redirects already followed (fails outright beyond 5)
ok: true = written successfully and the final response is 2xx
`
  'http.http-dl-resolve': `
Resolve a Location into an absolute URL (http(s) absolute / root-relative / plain relative)
use-tls: whether the current request goes over https (decides the scheme of the result)
host: host name of the current request
port: port number of the current request
loc: raw Location header value (absolute URL, root-relative path starting with '/', or plain relative path)
out: the resolved absolute URL string
`
  'http.http-write-ok': `
Write bytes and fold the ?bool result into a bool success flag
p: target file path
data: bytes to write (content for fs.write-bytes)
ok: true = write succeeded (folds the ok of ?bool into true; nil/err both give false)
`
  'http.patch': `
Perform an HTTP PATCH request
url: full URL
body: request body
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.ping': `
ping — temporary probe to detect source vs embedded std loading
s: returns a fixed pong string, used to identify whether the source file or the embedded std library was loaded
`
  'http.post': `
Perform an HTTP POST request
url: full URL
body: request body
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.post-auth': `
Perform an HTTP POST request with Basic authentication
url str: full URL
body str: request body
user str: user name
password str: password
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.put': `
Perform an HTTP PUT request
url: full URL
body: request body
returns resp ?response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http.request.add-header': `
Add a custom header
Assembles name and value into 'Name: Value' and stores it in the headers array
name str: header field name
value str: header field value
ok bool: true=appended to headers and header-count advanced, false=the header count reached the HTTP-MAX-REQ-HEADERS limit
`
  'http.request.init': `
Initialize the request
method: HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, etc.)
url: full URL
body: request body (GET/DELETE may pass an empty string)
`
  'http.response.get-header': `
Get a specific header value (linear search)
name: header name (e.g. 'Content-Type')
returns val ?str: ok = header value, nil = not found
`
  'http.response.parse-headers': `
Split the headers string into header-names / header-values
Must be called after the headers field is filled (do / do-req call it automatically internally)
`
  'http.set-api-key': `
Add an API Key header to the request
req request: request struct
header-name str: header name (e.g. 'X-API-Key')
api-key str: API key
ok bool: true=header added, false=request header slots are full
`
  'http.set-basic-auth': `
Add an HTTP Basic authentication header to the request
req request: request struct
user str: user name
password str: password
ok bool: true=header added, false=request header slots are full
`
  'http.set-bearer-auth': `
Add a Bearer Token authentication header to the request
req request: request struct
token str: bearer token
ok bool: true=header added, false=request header slots are full
`
  'http.upload-put': `
Streaming file upload (PUT, raw binary body)
Uses native TLS/net, no dependence on external tools (curl etc.).
url str: full URL (http:// or https://)
token str: bearer token; when non-empty an Authorization: Bearer header is attached
file-path str: path of the file to upload (read entirely into content before sending)
ok bool: true=upload completed and the HTTP status code is 2xx; false when the file cannot be read or the status is not 2xx
`
  'http2.conn.close': 'Close the connection (handles TLS/plaintext automatically)'
  'http2.conn.init': 'Initialize the connection state'
  'http2.conn.recv-frame': `
Receive a frame from the socket
returns frame ?frame: ok = received frame, nil = failure
`
  'http2.conn.request': `
Perform an HTTP/2 request
method: 'GET'/'POST'/'PUT'/'DELETE'/'PATCH'
path: request path
host: host name (used for the :authority header)
body: request body (empty string = no body)
returns resp = response, ok = true on success
names: output array of response header names (at most header-count entries)
values: output array of response header values (paired with names)
header-count: number of response headers actually parsed
resp-body: response body (DATA frame contents concatenated)
resp-n: byte length of the response body
ok: true = request completed cleanly, false = connection/parsing failed
`
  'http2.conn.send-data': `
Send a DATA frame
stream-id: stream id the DATA frame belongs to
data: content placed into the DATA frame payload
end-stream: true = append the END_STREAM flag (body fully sent)
ok: true = sent successfully
`
  'http2.conn.send-frame': `
Send a frame to the socket
returns ok = true on success
frame: frame to send (serialize writes out the 9-byte header and payload)
ok: true = written to the socket, false = write error
`
  'http2.conn.send-headers': `
Send a HEADERS frame
stream-id i64: stream identifier this HEADERS frame belongs to
names [64]str: array of header field names, paired by index with values
values [64]str: array of header field values
count i64: number of valid header entries (only the first count pairs are encoded)
end-stream bool: true=attach the END_STREAM flag, meaning this stream sends no more data
ok bool: true=HPACK encoding and sending succeeded
`
  'http2.conn.send-settings': `
Send an empty SETTINGS frame
ack: true = carry FLAG-ACK (SETTINGS ACK for the peer's settings), false = send an empty SETTINGS with parameter settings
ok: true = sent successfully
`
  'http2.connect': `
Establish an HTTP/2 h2c connection
Sends the preface + SETTINGS, receives the server's SETTINGS and ACKs it
returns conn ?conn: ok = connection, nil = failure
host: target server host name or IP
port: target TCP port (h2c cleartext connection)
`
  'http2.connect-tls': `
Establish an HTTP/2 over TLS connection
Creates the TLS connection first, then sends the HTTP/2 preface + SETTINGS
returns conn ?conn: ok = connection, nil = failure
host: target server host name or IP
port: target TLS port (usually 443)
`
  'http2.delete': `
HTTP/2 DELETE request
returns resp ?http.response: ok = response, nil = failure
url: request address 'http://host:port/path'
`
  'http2.do': `
HTTP/2 h2c convenience function
method: 'GET'/'POST'/'PUT'/'DELETE'/'PATCH'
url: 'http://host:port/path' (port optional, defaults to 80)
body: request body (empty string = no body)
returns names/values/header-count = response headers, resp-body/resp-n = response body, ok = true on success
names: output array of response header names
values: output array of response header values (paired with names)
header-count: number of response headers actually parsed
resp-body: response body
resp-n: byte length of the response body
ok: true = request completed cleanly, false = connection/parsing failed
`
  'http2.do-req': `
HTTP/2 request, returning ?http.response
method: 'GET'/'POST'/'PUT'/'DELETE'/'PATCH'
url: 'http://host:port/path'
body: request body (empty string = no body)
returns resp ?http.response: ok = response, nil = failure
`
  'http2.frame.parse': `
Parse a 9-byte frame header from data at pos
returns next-pos ?i64: ok = next position, nil = error
data: HTTP/2 byte stream (one 9-byte frame header parsed starting at pos)
pos: starting offset of this frame header within data
next-pos: ok = offset after the header, nil = insufficient data / parse failure
`
  'http2.frame.serialize': `
Serialize a frame header into buf at pos
returns next-pos = next position
buf: output buffer (the 9-byte frame header written at buf[pos..])
pos: starting offset of this frame header within buf
next-pos: next writable offset after serialization completes
`
  'http2.get': `
HTTP/2 GET request
returns resp ?http.response: ok = response, nil = failure
url: request address 'http://host:port/path' (port optional, defaults to 80)
