  'int.div': `
integer division, returns the quotient (a ÷ b)
returns err when b is 0 (the zero-divide guard the compiler inserts; this is not UB). See docs/docs/lang/syntax.md, "division by zero"
b int: divisor; the type must match the dividend; the sign follows C truncation toward zero (-7 / 2 = -3)
q ?int: ok=quotient (type same as the receiver), err=divisor is 0 (no nil case)
`
  'int.mod': `
integer modulo, returns the remainder (a % b)
returns err when b is 0 (the zero-divide guard the compiler inserts; this is not UB). See docs/docs/lang/syntax.md, "division by zero"
b int: divisor (must not be 0); the remainder's sign follows the dividend (-7 % 2 = -1)
r ?int: ok=remainder (|r| < |b|), err=divisor is 0 (no nil case)
`
  'int.to-str': `
converts an integer to a string (a union-type method covering every integer type)
the transpiler resolves i64.to-str() and friends to int.to-str()
it uses arithmetic directly, avoiding the int -> u64 conversion problem
note: it does not take the absolute value of negatives (0 - n overflows when n = i64.MIN);
instead it applies srem/sdiv to the negative value (truncating toward zero) and maps the negative remainder into [0, 9]
out str: decimal representation string, e.g. 42 / -17; zero prints '0', negatives carry a leading '-'
`
  'io.err': `
writes a string to standard error (no newline)
