  'json.json-clone-tree': `
Why a deep copy is required: json.pool is a #{inline=false} pointer field, and the compiler's pointer-field
Copying still lacks borrow semantics (see the copy-tree comment and the hybrid-ownership notes in src/mir),
Doing `child = json { pool: .pool, ... }` directly leaves parent and child each holding a vec header but sharing the same
the data buffer; once any handle triggers a vec realloc, the other handle's vec header points to freed
the freed old buffer → dangling read (stringify returns null / garbled data). After switching to an independent deep copy,
each side's alloc/growth writes its own buffer without affecting the other.
src: the source pool
idx: index of the subtree root within src
out: a new json holding a copied subtree (out.root=new root index; root=-1 when idx is invalid)
`
  'json.json-pool.add-child': `
attaches the child node child-idx (key name key; pass the empty string for arrays) to node node-idx
Implements the variable-length child list as a linked edge table, so no node has a fixed child limit
node-idx: index of the parent node (a new edge is appended to its first/last linked lists)
child-idx: child node index
key: the key on the edge (for object children; arrays pass an empty string)
`
  'json.json-pool.alloc': `
allocates a new node and returns its index (the heap vec grows automatically, so the pool is no longer bounded)
idx: index of the new node (kind=null, no children; the heap vec grows automatically and cannot fail)
`
  'json.json-pool.arr-get': `
gets the child node at an index of an array node
returns val-idx = the child node index, ok = success
arr-idx: array node index
elem: the element ordinal (walk the edge list to the elem-th edge)
val-idx: ok=the child node index, nil=not an array / invalid index / out of range
`
  'json.json-pool.arr-len': `
gets the array length
arr-idx: node index
n: number of children (returned for non-arrays too; 0 when the index is invalid)
`
  'json.json-pool.copy-tree': `
Pool identity cannot rely on `src.id == .id`: module-level bindings are not rebindable (see the JSON-POOL-NEXT-ID comment),
the id of every pool is always 1; this check once mistook every cross-pool copy as same-pool, so
the subtree root returned by json.arr-get / json.get was always -1. Callers now guarantee instead:
- json-clone-tree: the receiver is a brand-new pool straight out of init;
- json.set-key: first copies val into a temporary new pool (json-clone-tree), then copies directly from the relay pool
into the receiver; the two copies' ends must be in different pools, so self-references are safe too.
src: the source pool (must not be the receiver's own pool, otherwise the shared vec buffer breaks)
node-idx: index of the subtree root in the source pool
dst-idx: the new root index in the receiver pool (-1=source index invalid)
`
  'json.json-pool.get-bool': `
gets the boolean value
returns val = the boolean, ok = success
node-idx: node index
val: ok=bool-val, nil=not a boolean node
`
  'json.json-pool.get-key': `
gets the index of the child node for a key of an object node
returns val-idx = the child node index, ok = found
obj-idx: index of the object node
key: the key name
val-idx: ok=the child node index, nil=not an object / invalid index / key miss
`
  'json.json-pool.get-kind': `
gets the node kind
node-idx: node index
kind: JSON-KIND-* constant (NULL when the index is invalid)
`
  'json.json-pool.get-num': `
gets the numeric value
node-idx: node index
val: ok=num-val (integers and fractions both stored as f64), nil=not a number node
`
  'json.json-pool.get-str': `
gets the string value
returns out = a copy of the string, ok = success
node-idx: node index
out: ok=a copy of the string value, nil=not a string node or invalid index
`
  'json.json-pool.init': 'initialises the node pool (the heap vec has to be initialised to non-nil before use so that push is safe)'
  'json.json-pool.parse': `
parses a JSON value starting at position pos of the string
returns node-idx = the root node index, next-pos = the following position, ok = success
s: JSON string
pos: byte position where this value starts
node-idx: index of the root node (-1 on failure)
next-pos: the byte position following the value
ok: true=parsed successfully
`
  'json.json-pool.parse-num': `
parses a JSON number
