  'markdown.blockquote': `
Remove the leading > marker of a quote line.
if a single space follows `>` it is stripped as well; nested forms like `>>` keep the remaining `>`.
line str: the raw single line of text
out markdown-quote: yes means the line is a quote line, text is the content after removing the line-start marker (empty string for non-quote lines)
`
  'markdown.code-close': `
Determine whether the current line closes the given fence.
matches CommonMark: closing requires the same marker type and a length no smaller than the opening fence (at least 3).
line str: the single line to test
marker i64: the opening fence marker byte (96 or 126)
width i64: the length of the opened grid
yes bool: true means the line closes the current code block
`
  'markdown.escape-html': `
escapes HTML text and attribute values (the four characters & < > "; & must be replaced first or later replacements get double-escaped).
s str: raw text (body or attribute value)
out str: escaped result ready to splice into HTML
`
  'markdown.fence': `
parses the fenced code block marker.
after trimming, the opening fence line must start with 3 or more backticks or tildes; the rest of the text is the language label.
line str: the raw single line of text
out markdown-fence: yes means it is a fence line; marker is 96 (`) or 126 (~); width is the fence length; info is the language tag, empty string when absent
`
  'markdown.heading': `
Get the ATX heading level and heading text.
Accepts only the form "1–6 #'s plus one space", and strips the optional trailing "space + run of #".
line str: the raw single line of text
out markdown-heading: level is 1–6 (0 when not a heading), text is the heading content with the prefix and trailing # removed
`
  'markdown.inline': `
Render Markdown inline syntax.
Supports bold, italic, strikethrough, inline code, links, images, autolinks and backslash escapes; all text is HTML-escaped, and unclosed markers get their closing tags appended automatically at the end.
s str: Markdown text within a single line (do not pass block-level syntax)
out str: inline HTML fragment (without the enclosing <p>)
`
  'markdown.is-word-byte': `
Determine whether an ASCII byte belongs to a word.
c i64: the byte value to test (0–255, passed directly as an integer, not a char)
yes bool: true means c is 0-9 / A-Z / a-z; non-ASCII and punctuation are all false
`
  'markdown.join-paragraph': `
appends a line to the paragraph, handling hard line breaks specially.
when hard-break is true, the two trailing spaces are treated as <br>, stripped from the content, then joined with '<br>\n'.
paragraph str: paragraph text accumulated so far (empty string means a new paragraph)
line str: the raw line to append; with hard-break its end must carry two spaces
hard-break bool: true means the line ends with two spaces and a hard line break must be inserted
out str: paragraph text after the append
`
  'markdown.list-content': `
Render task-list content.
recognizes GFM task markers '[ ] ' and '[x] ' / '[X] '; the rest renders with inline syntax.
content str: the list item body (from markdown-list's text)
out str: HTML fragment for a disabled checkbox, or plain inline HTML
`
  'markdown.list-item': `
parses a list item. kind=1 unordered, kind=2 ordered.
Unordered items need "- / * / + plus one space"; ordered items need "at least one digit + . plus one space".
line str: the raw single line of text
out markdown-list: kind is 0 (not a list item) / 1 (unordered) / 2 (ordered), text is the entry content after the marker
`
  'markdown.prefix-count': `
counts the run of the given character at line start.
compares byte by byte from 0, stopping at the first differing character or the end of the string.
s str: the line to scan (usually already trimmed)
c i64: the byte value to compare (e.g. 35 for '#', 96 for '`')
n i64: prefix length; 0 when nothing matches
`
  'markdown.read-line': `
Read one line from the byte cursor, avoiding unsafe dynamic indexing into []str.
reads until '\n' or the end of the string, not including the newline.
source str: the entire Markdown source text
pos i64: current read position (byte offset, starting from 0)
out markdown-line: text=content of the line; next-pos=start offset of the next line; valid whether pos falls within [0, len)
`
  'markdown.render': `
A semantic alias of to-html, producing identical output.
source str: the complete Markdown source text
out str: a complete HTML document fragment
`
  'markdown.render-code': `
emits the code block.
when info is non-empty it becomes class="language-<info>" on the <code>, and the code content is fully HTML-escaped.
code str: the code body accumulated inside the fence (fence lines excluded)
info str: the language tag at the end of the fence line, empty string means no language
out str: a <pre><code…></code></pre>\n fragment
`
  'markdown.setext-level': `
Get the Setext heading level (=== or ---).
after trimming, the line must be at least 3 chars long and consist entirely of the same '=' or '-'.
line str: the raw single line of text
level i64: 1 = '=' underline (heading level 1), 2 = '-' underline (heading level 2), 0 = not a Setext heading
`
  'markdown.table-row': `
Render |-separated lines as tr.
strips leading and trailing '|' automatically; each cell is rendered with inline syntax and wrapped in <tag>.
line str: a single line of the table
tag str: the cell tag name, 'th' for header rows and 'td' for data rows
out str: a complete <tr>…</tr>\n fragment
`
  'markdown.table-separator': `
Determine a table separator row.
After splitting on '|', each cell must be empty once ':' and '-' are removed, and there must be at least one cell.
line str: the single line to test (usually the row right under the header)
yes bool: true means this is a table separator row like |---|:--:|
`
  'markdown.thematic-break': `
Determine whether it is a divider line.
After trimming, made of 3 or more of the same - / * / _, spaces and tabs allowed in between.
line str: the raw single line of text
yes bool: true means the line is a topic divider (<hr>)
`
  'markdown.to-html': `
Render a Markdown document as HTML.
First normalizes CRLF / CR to LF, then parses block-level syntax line by line (headings, lists, quotes, code blocks, tables, dividers, paragraphs).
source str: the complete Markdown source text
out str: a complete HTML document fragment
`
  'math.arr-zero': `
[n]num.zero / []num.zero — zeroes the storage with llvm.memset
fixed-size array: total bytes = n * sizeof(num), a compile-time constant
heap slice: .len() * sizeof(num) is taken at run time and passed to llvm.memset
This item takes no parameters and no results: codegen rewrites it into memset by the receiver type; the function body is only a placeholder
`
  'math.atan-kernel': `
atan2: arctangent y/x (pure .no implementation, avoids the libm dependency)
Internal atan-kernel: first reduces |t| to <= tan(pi/8), then uses a Taylor series
t f64: the normalized input value; when |t| is too large the series converges slowly and precision drops
r f64: approximate atan value (radians, range about (-pi/2, pi/2))
`
  'math.char-to-str': `
converts a char (Unicode code point) into a UTF-8 encoded string
replaces the old sprintf %c (which only handled single-byte ASCII)
⚠️ DEPRECATED: prefer the method form char.to-str() (see std/char.no),
e.g. c char = 'A'; c.to-str(); this function is kept only for existing call sites.
c char: the Unicode code point to encode
out str: the 1–4 byte UTF-8 encoding of the code point (capacity reserved with with-cap(4); length set by the actual bytes written)
`
  'math.clamp': `
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
integer maths functions (built-in)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
clamp: constrains a value to [min, max]
Compares the upper bound before the lower bound: when min > max the result is min (the later of the two selects wins)
val i64: the value to clamp
min i64: the lower limit; results smaller than it are replaced by min
max i64: the upper limit; results greater than it are replaced by max
result i64: an i64 within [min, max] (min when min > max)
`
  'math.div': `
-7 / 2 == -3 (not -4: for floor semantics use the sign check yourself)
⚠️ when b == 0 it traps just like a bare `/` (there is no run-time zero-divide check).
a int: the dividend
b int: divisor, must not be 0
r int: quotient truncated toward zero
`
  'math.even': `
whether the value is even — works for every integer type (unsigned included)
a int: the integer to test
yes bool: true means a is divisible by 2 (0 is even)
`
  'math.fmod': `
miscellaneous
Float remainder res = x - trunc(x/y) * y (C's fmod semantics)
x f64: the dividend
y f64: the divisor; when y == 0.0 the result is NaN
res f64: remainder carrying the sign of x with |res| < |y|
`
  'math.gcd': `
greatest common divisor (Euclid's algorithm)
Both numbers take absolute values before iterating, so the result is always non-negative (gcd(0, 0) = 0)
a int: the first integer
b int: the second integer
r int: greatest common divisor of a and b, non-negative
`
  'math.hypot': `
the Euclidean norm sqrt(x*x + y*y)
x f64: the first leg of the right angle
y f64: the second leg of the right angle
res f64: hypotenuse length; since it squares then adds, intermediates overflow to inf when |x| or |y| nears the f64 limit
`
  'math.lcm': `
least common multiple
Computed as a / gcd(a, b) * b; dividing before multiplying reduces intermediate overflow
a int: the first integer
b int: the second integer; the result is 0 when either operand is 0
r int: least common multiple of a and b, non-negative; product overflow wraps per #{overflow=wrap}
`
  'math.max': `
Returns the maximum of the actual argument sequence (variadic generic, works for all member types of num)
a ..num: the numeric sequence to compare; pass at least one element (an empty sequence indexes out of range)
r num: the maximum element of the sequence, typed like a's members
`
  'math.min': `
Returns the minimum of the actual argument sequence (variadic generic, works for all member types of num)
a ..num: the numeric sequence to compare; pass at least one element (an empty sequence indexes out of range)
r num: the minimum element of the sequence, typed like a's members
`
  'math.mod': `
-7 % 2 == -1 (for floor-mod semantics use the sign check yourself)
⚠️ when b == 0 it traps just like a bare `%`.
a int: the dividend
b int: divisor, must not be 0
r int: remainder with |r| < |b| and the same sign as a
`
  'math.odd': `
whether the value is odd — works for every integer type (unsigned included)
a int: the integer to test
yes bool: true means a is not divisible by 2 (negative odd numbers are also true)
`
  'math.pow': `
⚠️ Known limitations (differences from C pow, all at extreme boundaries):
1. when |y| > 2^63 f64-to-i64 overflows and the parity test is wrong ⇒ the sign of inf may flip.
Here mag itself is already inf/0 and only affects the sign.
2. when x==0.0 and y<0, C pow returns +inf; this implementation returns 0.0 (keeps the old behavior).
x f64: the base, may be negative (negative bases take the sign-correction branch below)
y f64: the exponent; when x<0 y must be an integer, otherwise the result is NaN
res f64: x raised to the power y; 0^0 = 1.0, and every other x==0.0 case (including y<0) yields 0.0
`
  'math.pow-int': `
pow: integer power a^n (n >= 0) — works for every integer type
Uses fast exponentiation, O(log n)
⚠️ not moved into math: the name collides with std/math.no's pow (the f64 power function),
and one module cannot hold two pow definitions; this is the single exception to the rule that function-form APIs live in math.
a int: base
n int: exponent; when n <= 0 the loop never runs and the result is fixed at 1
r int: a raised to the power n; multiplications wrap via #{overflow=wrap}, no overflow reported
`
  'math.rotate-left': `
[]num.zero = () {
i = 0
for i < .len() {
.[i] = 0
i = i + 1
}
}
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
bit operations (built-in)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
rotate-left: rotates an integer left by n bits (mapped onto ARM64 ROR via llvm.fshl)
x i64: the 64-bit integer to rotate
n i64: rotate-left amount, modulo 64 (n may be negative, equivalent to rotating right by |n|)
r i64: the result after rotating left
`
  'math.rotate-right': `
rotates an integer right by n bits (mapped onto ARM64 ROR via llvm.fshr)
x i64: the 64-bit integer to rotate
n i64: rotate-right amount, modulo 64 (n may be negative, equivalent to rotating left by |n|)
r i64: the result after rotating right
`
  'math.store-le-u32': `
writes a u32 value in little-endian at the given offset of a byte array
arr i64: start address of the target byte array (passed as i64)
offset i64: byte offset of the write position relative to arr; the caller must ensure offset+4 stays in range
val u32: the 32-bit value to write, the low 8 bytes land first
`
  'math.swap': `
Swap two values: exchange a and b using a temporary variable, generic t
a t: first output parameter (holds the original value before the call, holds b's original value on return)
b t: second output parameter (holds the original value before the call, holds a's original value on return)
`
  'md5.md5': `
Compute the MD5 hash (RFC 1321; input of any length, padded internally to a multiple of 512 bits)
data []byte: input bytes to digest
out [16]byte: 128-bit digest, stored in MD5's standard little-endian word order
`
  'md5.md5-rotl': `
32-bit left rotation. math.rotate-left is 64-bit wide and does not fit MD5's u32 round functions,
so a true 32-bit rotation is implemented manually with shifts plus a mask (0xFFFFFFFF).
x u32: 32-bit value to rotate
n i64: left-rotation amount in bits (1..31; 32 would overflow the shift amount)
r u32: rotation result, already & 0xFFFFFFFF
`
