pos: start position of the literal
idx: node index; -1 = failure
next: position after the value
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-num': `
Parse a number literal (integer / decimal / scientific notation, leading '-' allowed, '+' not accepted)
Consumes only the bytes that form the number itself, stopping at any other character; no separator required
s str: full source text
pos i64: byte index where the number starts
val f64: parsed value (NOML has one number type; integers are also stored as f64)
next i64: byte index after the number (on failure stops at the error position for .fail to mark)
ok bool: true = parsed, false = syntax error (no integer part, no fraction after '.', no digits in exponent)
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-num-node': `
Parse a number value and materialize it as a node
s: source text
pos: start position of the number
idx: node index; -1 = failure
next: position after the value
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-object': `
Parse an object `{ member... }` (members separated by newlines, no commas)
s: source text
pos: position of `{`
idx: object node index; -1 = failure
next: position after `}`
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-raw': `
Parse a backtick raw string (the opening ` must be followed by a newline; the closing ` must be alone on its line)
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
val: verbatim content (no escape parsing)
next: position after the closing backtick
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-raw-node': `
Parse a raw string value and materialize it as a node
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
idx: node index; -1 = failure
next: position after the value
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-str': `
Parse a single-quoted string (with `\\` escapes)
Built by concatenating segments rather than a byte buffer: each escape point cuts a segment, and segments plus decoded results are concatenated directly, so no capacity estimate is needed and no buffer-view lifetime issues arise.
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
val: the decoded string
next: position after the closing quote
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-str-node': `
Parse a string value and materialize it as a node
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
idx: node index; -1 = failure
next: position after the value
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.parse-value': `
Parse a value
s: source text
pos: start position of the value
idx: node index; -1 = failure
next: position after the value
ok: true = parsed successfully
`
  'noml.noml-pool.quote': `
Produce a single-quoted string literal (with escapes)
Escaping rules: '\'' and '\\' get a preceding backslash, LF / CR / TAB are written as \n \r \t,
other control bytes below 0x20 are written as \u00XX; the output buffer reserves n*6+2 (worst case 6 characters per byte).
s str: raw content (any bytes allowed, valid UTF-8 not required)
out str: literal wrapped in single quotes with the interior escaped
`
  'noml.noml-pool.quote-value': `
Produce a string literal in the simplest form suitable for the content
Defaults to .quote(); when the content contains LF and passes .raw-safe(), a backtick raw string is used, preserving the original newlines.
s str: raw content
out str: text literal ready to write into a .noml file (single-quoted escaped form or '`' raw string)
`
  'noml.noml-pool.raw-safe': `
Decide whether the content can be put into a backtick raw string and retrieved verbatim
Unusable when any of these occur: empty string, first or last byte is LF, contains CR,
or any line (after stripping leading blanks: space / TAB / CR) starts with '`' (it would be mistaken for the terminator).
s str: content to serialize
yes bool: true = can be safely wrapped as '`\n' + s + '\n`', false = the single-quoted escaped form must be used
`
  'noml.noml-pool.require-end': `
Member-end validation: only intra-line blanks, newline, `;` comment, or container close may follow
s: source text
pos: position after the value
key: member key name (used only in error messages)
next: position after the newline (or `}`)
ok: true = legal
`
  'noml.noml-pool.scan-ann': `
Scan one `#{...}` group, returning the position after `}` (validates structure only, does not parse entries)
s: source text
pos: position of `#`
next: position after `}`; -1 = not an annotation or unclosed
`
  'noml.noml-pool.scan-quote': `
Scan from `'` to just past the next unescaped `'`
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
next: position after the closing quote; -1 = unclosed
`
  'noml.noml-pool.scan-raw': `
Scan from a backtick to just past the closing backtick that is alone on its line
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
next: position after the closing backtick; -1 = unclosed
`
  'noml.noml-pool.set-ann': `
Set the node's raw `#{...}` annotation text (empty string = remove it)
idx: node index
text: annotation text (without `#{` and `}`)
ok: true = written
`
  'noml.noml-pool.skip-ann-value': `
Skip one annotation value (string / array / bare value), returning the position after it
body: annotation text
pos: value start position
next: position after the value; -1 = malformed
`
  'noml.noml-pool.skip-blanks': `
Skip only intra-line blanks (spaces and tabs), not newlines
s: source text
pos: byte offset to start scanning from
p: position of the first non-blank character
`
  'noml.noml-pool.skip-trivia': `
Skip whitespace (including newlines) and `;` line comments
s: source text
pos: byte offset to start scanning from
p: position of the first meaningful character (end of string when all blanks)
`
  'noml.noml-pool.stringify': `
Serialize a subtree
idx: subtree root index
indent: indentation level (two spaces per level)
out: NOML text (empty string when the index is invalid)
`
