  'noml.parse': `
Parse NOML text
Usage:
r = noml.parse('{\nname: \'nolang\'\n}')
r: {
ok -> print(it.stringify())
err -> print(it)
}
s: full NOML text (leading and trailing whitespace is skipped)
result: ok = parsed document, err (message with line number) = syntax error
`
  'num.abs': `
absolute value — works for every num type (integers and floats)
call form: abs(x) (x is the receiver; a zero-argument num method currently supports only this form, because
x.abs() dot form is not supported — the nested num union does not monomorphise the receiver yet — same as num.sign);
negatives wrap under overflow=wrap (the absolute value of i64.MIN is out of i64 range)
r num: non-negatives returned unchanged; negatives return the negated value (the most negative integer wraps back to itself, staying negative)
`
  'num.clamp': `
clamps into [lo, hi]
usage: x.clamp(lo, hi, r)
Ordered short-circuit judgment: lower bound first, then upper bound. So when lo > hi (reversed interval) the lower bound wins,
Values below lo are raised to lo; values above hi are pushed down to hi.
lo num: the lower bound (inclusive); inputs smaller than lo yield lo
hi num: the upper bound (inclusive); inputs greater than hi yield hi
r num: the clamped value, inside [lo, hi] (when the interval is reversed, one of {lo, hi})
`
  'num.sign': `
the sign (-1, 0, 1) — works for every num type (integers and floats)
Accepts ±0.0 and the various integer types; NaN falls to 0 because both comparisons are false.
r num: 1=greater than zero, -1=less than zero, 0=equal to zero (±0.0 included; NaN takes the else branch)
`
  'number.byte.to-str': `
converts a byte to a string (unsigned; widened to u64 and reused)
out: decimal representation (byte as unsigned 0..255, formatted after widening to u64)
`
  'number.exp-f64': `
Exponentials and logarithms (f64/f32 methods)
free-function form (takes parameter x rather than a method receiver).
Serves as the single implementation source for f64.exp / cbrt / sinh / cosh / f32.exp.
Note: Nolang currently has an issue with "method calls on named region variables" (like t.exp())
codegen bug would wrongly use the outer receiver; so every indirect call of exp goes through this
a free function, avoiding that problem.
x: the exponent (f64)
res: e raised to the power x (x>709 returns +Inf, x<-745 returns 0.0; range halving then a z⁸ Taylor series)
`
  'number.f32.to-f64': `
widens an f32 to f64
out: the equivalent f64 (exact widening, no precision loss)
`
  'number.f32.to-str': `
converts an f32 to a string (method)
Widens through f32.to-f64 (a #{buildin}) and reuses the Nolang %g implementation of f64.to-str.
out: decimal string (widened to f64 then through the f64 %g shortest round-trip implementation)
`
  'number.f64.cbrt': `
cube root (f64 method)
Implemented with exp(log(|x|) / 3); the method body does not call pow (integer power pow is in this module, f64 pow is in std/math.no)
res: cube root of x (implemented as exp(log(|x|)/3) carrying the sign; 0 returns 0.0)
`
  'number.f64.ceil': `
Rounding (f64/f32 methods; on integer types rounding is the identity, so no generic version is offered)
res: the smallest integer value not less than x (rounds up, result still f64)
`
  'number.f64.degrees': `
radians to degrees (f64/f32 method)
res: x converted from radians to degrees (× 180/π)
`
  'number.f64.radians': `
degrees to radians (f64/f32 method)
res: x converted from degrees to radians (× π/180)
`
  'number.f64.sin': `
Trigonometric functions (radians; f64/f32 methods)
res: sin(x) (x in radians, polynomial approximation)
`
  'number.f64.sinh': `
Hyperbolic functions (f64/f32 methods)
res: sinh(x) = (eˣ - e⁻ˣ)/2
`
  'number.f64.sqrt': `
Powers and roots (f64/f32 methods)
res: √x (native libm sqrt; negatives return NaN)
`
  'number.f64.tan': `
tangent (libm tan, equivalent to sin/cos)
res: tan(x) = sin(x)/cos(x) (diverges where cos(x)≈0)
`
  'number.f64.to-bits': `
extracts the 64-bit IEEE-754 bit pattern of an f64 as a u64 (bitcast double->i64).
Registered as ForwardFunc "f64-to-bits" by src/builtin/bits.go, implemented as a native LLVM bitcast and independent of libm.
f64.to-str takes the bit pattern first, then splits sign/exp/frac, before expanding the shortest round-trip form.
out: the 64-bit pattern of the IEEE-754 double (bitcast, not a numeric conversion)
`
  'number.f64.to-f32': `
narrows an f64 to f32 (may lose precision)
out: the narrowed f32 (may lose precision or overflow to ±Inf)
`
  'number.f64.to-i32': `
float to 32-bit signed integer (saturating)
out: the i32 truncated toward zero (out-of-range saturates)
`
  'number.f64.to-i64': `
float to integer (truncating)
out: the integer truncated toward zero (fraction dropped, e.g. 3.9→3, -3.9→-3)
`
  'number.f64.to-str': `
f64 to string (direct Nolang implementation, **shortest round-trip** decimal, aligned with Go's %v)
Takes "the fewest significant digits that uniquely recover this double" (IEEE-754 bit decomposition + bigint exact arithmetic),
And follows %g's notation choice: E <= -5 or E >= 6 uses scientific notation, otherwise fixed-point.
Special values align with Go: NaN → "NaN", +Inf → "+Inf", -Inf → "-Inf", ±0 → "0".
The backend has no native f64 formatter (the sprintf CLibCall was removed), so the whole routine is inlined in the method body.
out: shortest round-trip decimal string (fewest significant digits that uniquely recover this double; scientific notation when E≤-5 or E≥6; NaN/±Inf aligned with Go)
`
  'number.f64.to-str-fixed': `
fixed-point conversion
precision i64: number of fractional digits (>= 0; keep it <= 17 to avoid i64 overflow)
returns out str: the string form (e.g. "3.14", "-0.005", "100")
`
  'number.f64.to-str-general': `
general format (chooses fixed point or scientific notation automatically)
precision i64: maximum number of significant digits (>= 1)
returns out str: the string form (trailing zeros stripped automatically)
`
  'number.f64.to-str-sci': `
scientific notation
precision i64: number of fractional digits (>= 0)
returns out str: the string form (e.g. "3.14e+2", "-5.0e-3")
`
  'number.f64.to-u32': `
float to 32-bit unsigned integer (saturating)
out: the u32 truncated toward zero (out-of-range saturates)
`
  'number.f64.to-u64': `
float to 64-bit unsigned integer (saturating)
out: the u64 truncated toward zero (negatives and out-of-range saturate)
`
  'number.i128.sqrt': '── 128-bit integer method copies (i128 / u128) ──\n128-bit arithmetic itself works (operands go through the LLVM i128 path), but two points must be handled:\n① region variables need explicit types (`n i128 = .`, `lo i128 = 1` …). Without types,\nchecker infers intermediate values mixed with literals as i64, so the loop converges on a wrong value or even\nhangs (the binary search for i128.MAX behaves exactly so). Untyped u128 also errors on an i64→u128 assignment.\n② overflow-annotation layout: i128 (signed, division may overflow INT_MIN/-1) adds an\n`#{overflow=wrap}`; u128 (unsigned, ÷/% cannot overflow) only applies to + − and "division by a literal"\noperations get an annotation, while ÷/% between variables do not (otherwise overflow-ineffective warnings).\ncomments must be on their own line above the target statement (a guard chained with `;` on one line triggers\n128-bit codegen drops the whole main).\nAlso fixes codegen isUnsignedRaw omitting u128: comparisons/division of u128 previously went down the signed path by mistake\nsign predicate misorders the u128.MAX range (≥ 2^127); after the fix u128.sqrt(u128.MAX)=2^64-1 is exact.\nr: floor(√n) (128-bit; typed region variable + overflow-wrap annotation)'
  'number.i128.to-f64': `
128-bit signed integer to float
out: the equivalent f64 (precision lost when the 128-bit magnitude is large)
`
  'number.i16.sqrt': `
i16
r: floor(√n) (signed, returns 0 when n<0)
`
  'number.i16.to-str': `
converts an i16 to a string (widened to i64 and reused)
out: decimal representation (formatted after widening to i64)
`
  'number.i32.sqrt': `
i32
r: floor(√n) (signed, returns 0 when n<0)
`
  'number.i32.to-str': `
converts an i32 to a string (implicitly widened to i64, then reusing the native i64.to-str path)
out: decimal representation (implicitly widened to i64 then reusing the native path)
`
  'number.i64.is-prime': `
Whether it is prime — an i64 method
trial division O(√n): rule out evens first, then try odd divisors; termination uses d <= n/d
avoids d*d overflow; the divisor is always ≥ 1 so it never traps (likewise no int union receiver, see above)
yes: whether it is prime (always false for n<2; divisible only by 1 and itself)
`
  'number.i64.sqrt': 'Integer square root floor(√n) — an i64 method\nn < 0 has no solution in the integer domain and returns 0; binary search O(log n), comparing with n/mid rather than\nmid*mid, no overflow (following the wrap-annotation convention of gcd/even in math.no).\nDoes not use an int union receiver (which would work across all widths): the int alias is defined in std/int.no,\ndeclaring an `int.X` method across modules gets double-prefixed by prefixMethodNames into\n`int.int.X`; the call site\'s monomorphization finds no match and reports unknown callee.\nr: floor(√n) (n<0 returns 0; n<2 returns n itself; binary search O(log n))'
  'number.i64.to-bytes-be': `
converts an int64 to 8 big-endian bytes
usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-be()   // out = [0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x12,0x34]
out: an 8-byte array (high byte first, big endian)
`
  'number.i64.to-bytes-le': `
converts an int64 to 8 little-endian bytes
usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-le()   // out = [0x34,0x12,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00]
out: an 8-byte array (low byte first, little endian)
`
  'number.i64.to-f64': `
