  'str-set.str-set.add': `
Adds an element; returns whether it was inserted
linear probing finds a slot; when the same string is hit, no duplicate insert happens.
with the table full and the string absent, probing loops forever; the caller must keep the element count under control.
key str: the string to add (copied into the slot)
is-new bool: true=it was absent and got inserted, false=already existed, unchanged
`
  'str-set.str-set.clear': 'Clears the set'
  'str-set.str-set.contains': `
Reports whether an element exists
key str: the string to query
found bool: true=the string exists in the set, false=absent (returns immediately when the set is empty)
`
  'str-set.str-set.for-each': `
Iterates over every element
Walks [0, cap) in slot-index order (not insertion order), assigning each occupied slot's string to val in turn.
This language has no callbacks; when the function returns, val stops at the element of the last non-empty slot.
val str: the string of the current slot (the last assignment)
`
  'str-set.str-set.hash': `
FNV-1a string hash
key str: the string to hash (walked byte by byte)
idx i64: slot index within [0, cap) (hash & (cap-1), case-insensitive)
`
  'str-set.str-set.init': 'Initialises the set'
  'str-set.str-set.is-empty': `
Whether the set is empty
empty bool: true=size == 0, false=elements remain
`
  'str-set.str-set.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n i64: current number of strings present in the set
`
  'str-set.str-set.remove': `
Removes an element
Clears the slot's occ and keys[i].len without rearranging the probe chain; adding the same key again reuses the slot.
key str: the string to delete
removed bool: true=found and deleted, false=absent
`
  'str-set.str-set.str-eq': `
Compares two strs for equality
Compares length first for fast rejection, then matches byte by byte (no case-insensitive handling).
a str: left operand
b str: right operand
eq bool: true=same length and every byte identical, false=unequal
`
