`
  'str.byte': `
returns the raw byte value at byte index i (raw byte read)
unlike s[i]: s[i] indexes by code point, this method reads by byte offset into the underlying UTF-8 buffer
Returns ?byte: out of range (i < 0 or i >= len-bytes()) returns nil, consistent with arr[i]'s
Safe-indexing semantics are consistent; callers must handle the option (match / ?= / #{index-out = DEF})
Handled by the compiler builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts and expands it into "bounds check + GEP+load",
There is no std function body; the signature is also registered in BuiltinMethodList in src/builtin/str.go
(MethodName "byte", Intercepted, the bare-name fallback covers both str and txt)
i: byte index (not a code point)
c: ok=the raw byte at that position, nil=i out of range
`
  'str.byte-index': `
converts a code point (character) index to a byte offset
Returns the byte position where the cp-pos-th code point starts; when cp-pos is past the end, returns .len
cp-pos: code point (character) index
byte: the byte
`
  'str.compare': `
Compare two strings lexicographically
returns -1 (a<b), 0 (equal), 1 (a>b)
b: comparison target string
r: lexicographic -1 (receiver<b) / 0 (equal) / 1 (>)
`
  'str.contains': `
Determine whether s contains the substring target
returns true = contains, false = does not
target: the substring to find
yes: whether s contains target
`
  'str.copy': `
Copy src into dst (the whole string)
⚠️ DEPRECATED: assignment `dst = src` already has copy semantics — ownership analysis will, at src
If it is still read later it is automatically rewritten into a deep copy (OpClone); no manual copy needed. Kept only for compatibility.
dst: the copy (deprecated: plain assignment already has deep-copy semantics)
`
  'str.count': `
counts the chars (code points) in the string
