  'str.to-str': 'The generic []t.to-str / [n]t.to-str templates call `.[i].to-str()` per element; when the element type is str\nfalls through to this method. std lacked it before, and `[\'a\',\'b\'].to-str()` reported at build time\n`unknown callee str.to-str` (produced by the _xstr.to-str forwarding stub).\nstr is immutable, so returning itself suffices (value semantics: the underlying buffer is shared, not copied).\nout: itself (str is immutable; value semantics share the underlying buffer without copying)'
  'str.to-txt': `
Convert a str into a txt (at most 255 bytes)
returns out ?txt: ok(txt) / nil (beyond 255 bytes the txt cannot hold it; no more silent truncation)
`
  'str.to-u16': `
parses the string into a u16
returns Option:
ok(n) - success (0 .. 65535)
nil    - empty string
err('msg') - contains a non-digit character, a lone +/- , or is out of range
val: ok=a u16 in range (0~65535), nil=empty string, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'str.to-u32': `
parses the string into a u32
returns Option:
ok(n) - success (0 .. 4294967295)
nil    - empty string
err('msg') - contains a non-digit character, a lone +/- , or is out of range
val: ok=a u32 within range, nil=empty string, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'str.to-u64': `
parses the string into a u64
Rejects a minus sign; i64 range overflow is not checked (LLVM truncates automatically)
val: ok=a non-negative integer (rejects a sign), nil=empty string, err=contains non-digit characters
`
  'str.to-u8': `
parses the string into a u8
returns Option:
ok(n) - success (0 .. 255)
nil    - empty string
err('msg') - contains a non-digit character, a lone +/- , or is out of range
val: ok=a u8 in range (0~255), nil=empty string, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'str.to-upper': `
Convert a string to uppercase
returns the new string
val: a copy with ASCII lowercase converted to uppercase (other codepoints preserved as-is)
`
  'str.trim': `
Trim whitespace characters from both ends of the string
returns the new string
val: a copy with leading and trailing whitespace removed
`
  'str.trim-char': `
Trim specified characters (UTF-8 codepoints) from both ends of the string
ch: the code point to strip from both ends
val: a copy with leading and trailing ch removed
`
  'str.with-cap': `
creates a new string with the given capacity (len=0)
s = ''.with-cap(256)  -> an empty string pre-allocating 256 bytes
A convenience method, equivalent to the global with-cap(cap)
cap: pre-allocated capacity (bytes)
out: a new empty string with len=0 and cap allocated
`
  'str.with-len': `
creates a string of the given length
s = ''.with-len(10)  -> a string of length 10
A convenience method, equivalent to the global with-len(len)
len: initial length (bytes)
out: a new string of the given length
`
  'str.with-len-cap': `
creates a string with the given length and capacity
s = ''.with-len-cap(10, 256)  -> a string of length 10 and capacity 256
A convenience method, equivalent to the global with-cap-len(cap, len)
len: length (bytes)
cap: capacity (bytes)
out: a new string with the given length and capacity
`
  'strs.join': `
[]str.join: joins every element of a string slice with a separator
sep str: the separator
returns out str: the joined string
`
  'tar.tar-builder.add-bytes': `
Add a raw byte entry
name: entry name
content: entry content bytes
typ: entry type code (TAR-TYPE-FILE / TAR-TYPE-DIR)
`
  'tar.tar-builder.add-dir': `
Add a directory entry
name: directory name (conventionally ending with /, no content area)
`
  'tar.tar-builder.add-file': `
Add a file entry
name: file name (written to the header name field)
content: file content (written, then zero-padded to the 512-byte boundary)
`
  'tar.tar-builder.finish': `
Finish building, write the end marker and return the tar archive
archive: read-only tar view containing the two all-zero end blocks
`
  'tar.tar-builder.pre-alloc': `
Pre-allocate the buffer (push via a local variable to work around the struct-field push limitation)
size: number of bytes to pre-allocate in the buffer (zero-filled to this length)
`
  'tar.tar-builder.write-header': `
Write the 512-byte header (internal method)
name: file name (written at header offset 0)
typ: typeflag character code (48=file, 53=directory)
sz: content byte count (written to the size field in octal)
`
  'tar.tar-builder.write-octal': `
Write an octal number at the given offset (internal method)
offset: field offset within the current header block
width: field width (octal digits right-aligned, left padded with spaces)
val: integer value to encode (a NUL is appended at the block end)
`
  'tar.tar-entry.is-dir': `
Whether the entry is a directory
yes: whether this entry is a directory
`
  'tar.tar-entry.is-file': `
Whether the entry is a regular file
yes: whether this entry is a regular file
`
  'tar.tar-entry.mode': `
Read the entry permissions
mode: permission bits (octal decoded)
`
  'tar.tar-entry.mtime': `
Read the entry modification time
ts: modification time (Unix seconds, octal decoded)
`
  'tar.tar-entry.name': `
Read the entry file name
name: this entry's file name
`
  'tar.tar-entry.read': `
Extract the entry content
out: entry content bytes (empty for directories)
`
  'tar.tar-entry.size': `
Read the entry file size
sz: entry's raw byte count
`
  'tar.tar-entry.type': `
Read the entry type
typ: type string ("file", "dir" or "unknown")
`
  'tar.tar-open-gz': `
Create a tar view from gzip-compressed data (.tar.gz)
gz-data: gzip-compressed tar data ([]byte)
archive: the decompressed tar archive
`
  'tar.tar.count': `
Count the valid entries in the TAR
count: number of entries before the end marker (all-zero block)
`
  'tar.tar.entry': `
Get the idx-th entry
idx: entry ordinal
e: tar-entry pointing at its 512-byte header (fields read via its methods)
`
  'tar.tar.is-dir': `
Whether the idx-th entry is a directory
idx: entry ordinal
yes: whether it is a directory entry (typeflag 5)
`
  'tar.tar.is-file': `
Whether the idx-th entry is a regular file
idx: entry ordinal
yes: whether it is a regular file entry (typeflag 0)
`
  'tar.tar.mode': `
Read the idx-th entry's permissions (octal)
idx: entry ordinal
mode: file permission bits (mode field octal decoded, e.g. 0o644)
`
  'tar.tar.mtime': `
Read the idx-th entry's modification time (octal Unix timestamp)
idx: entry ordinal
ts: modification time (Unix seconds, mtime field octal decoded)
`
  'tar.tar.name': `
Read the idx-th entry's file name
idx: entry ordinal
name: file name (header name field, truncated at NUL/space)
`
  'tar.tar.offset': `
Compute the idx-th entry's actual byte offset (traversing all preceding entries)
idx: entry ordinal (0 ~ count-1)
off: starting byte offset of the entry's header within the archive data
`
  'tar.tar.read': `
Extract the idx-th entry's content
idx: entry ordinal
out: entry content bytes (read for the length given by the size field, empty for directories)
`
  'tar.tar.size': `
Read the idx-th entry's file size (octal)
idx: entry ordinal
sz: file's raw byte count (size field octal decoded)
`
  'tar.tar.type': `
Read the idx-th entry's type string
returns "file", "dir" or "unknown"
idx: entry ordinal
typ: entry type string (mapped from typeflag to "file", "dir" or "unknown")
`
  'tdes.tdes-cbc-dec': `
Triple DES-CBC decrypt
Each block is 3DES-ECB decrypted first, then XORed with the previous ciphertext segment (the iv for the first block) to recover plaintext.
in []byte: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 8)
key [24]byte: key (K1 || K2 || K3), must match the encryption key
iv [8]byte: initial vector, must be the same as during encryption
returns out []byte: decrypted data, same length as in
`
  'tdes.tdes-cbc-enc': `
Triple DES-CBC encrypt
Each block is XORed with the previous ciphertext block (the iv for the first) before 3DES-ECB,
so the same plaintext yields different ciphertext under different ivs.
in []byte: input data (length must be a multiple of 8)
key [24]byte: key (K1 || K2 || K3)
iv [8]byte: initial vector, should be unique per message
returns out []byte: encrypted data, same length as in
`
  'tdes.tdes-dec': `
Triple DES decrypt (ECB mode)
The inverse of encryption: D_K3 → E_K2 → D_K1; each 8-byte block is processed independently.
in []byte: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 8)
n i64: number of bytes actually processed (usually in.len())
key [24]byte: key (K1 || K2 || K3), must match the encryption key
returns out []byte: decrypted data, same length as n
`
  'tdes.tdes-enc': `
Triple DES encrypt (ECB mode)
Runs E_K1 → D_K2 → E_K3 on each 8-byte block; blocks are independent (same plaintext gives same ciphertext).
in []byte: input data (length must be a multiple of 8; a trailing partial block is not processed)
n i64: number of bytes actually processed (usually in.len())
key [24]byte: key (K1 || K2 || K3, 8 bytes each)
returns out []byte: encrypted data, same length as n
`
  'time.add-seconds': `
adds seconds and returns a new time
n: seconds to add (may be negative)
t: the new time (sec+n, nsec unchanged)
`
  'time.date': `
converts to a date struct (UTC, nsec ignored)
d: the UTC date struct corresponding to .sec
`
  'time.date-to-unix': `
converts a date struct (UTC) into a Unix timestamp (seconds)
Uses the days-from-civil algorithm (Howard Hinnant)
d: the UTC date struct
ts: the Unix timestamp (seconds) of the corresponding moment
`
  'time.days-in-month-fn': `
_days-in-month: gets the number of days in the given year and month
y: the year (used for the leap-year test)
m: the month (1..12)
d: days in that month (February returns 29/28 by leap year; returns 31 when m is out of range)
`
  'time.duration-between': `
computes the difference between two timestamps (seconds)
start: starting timestamp (seconds)
end: the end timestamp (seconds)
diff: end - start (seconds, wrapping on overflow)
`
  'time.duration-ms-between': `
computes the difference between two timestamps (milliseconds)
start: starting timestamp (milliseconds)
end: the end timestamp (milliseconds)
diff: end - start (milliseconds, wrapping on overflow)
`
  'time.duration-time': `
computes the difference between two time values (seconds)
start: starting time point
end: the end point in time
diff: the difference of both sec fields (seconds, nsec ignored)
`
  'time.duration-us-between': `
computes the difference between two timestamps (microseconds)
start: starting timestamp (microseconds)
end: the end timestamp (microseconds)
diff: end - start (microseconds, wrapping on overflow)
`
  'time.format': `
formats as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' (UTC, nsec ignored)
out: a 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' string
`
  'time.format-date': `
formats a date struct into a string
Format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS (a negative year gets a '-' prefix)
d: the date struct to format
out: a 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' string (each field zero-padded)
`
  'time.format-duration': `
formats a number of seconds into a human-readable string
e.g. 3661 -> '1h1m1s'
seconds: duration (seconds)
out: an 'NhHmMs' style string (all three segments always shown, e.g. 60 → '0h1m0s'; negatives not handled)
`
  'time.format-time-str': `
formats a Unix timestamp into a readable string
Format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS (UTC)
ts: Unix timestamp (seconds)
out: formatted UTC time string
`
  'time.is-leap': `
tests whether a year is a leap year
y: the year
leap: whether it is a leap year (divisible by 4 and not by 100, or divisible by 400)
`
  'time.now-date': `
gets the current date-time struct (UTC)
d: the current UTC date and time
`
  'time.now-ms': `
the current Unix timestamp (milliseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (milliseconds)
`
  'time.now-ns': `
the current timestamp (nanoseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (nanoseconds)
`
  'time.now-s': `
the current Unix timestamp (seconds)
Wraps the builtin now()
ts: the current Unix timestamp (seconds)
`
  'time.now-time': `
the current instant (UTC)
Splits sec and nsec out of the builtin now-ns() (CLOCK_REALTIME nanoseconds)
t: the current time point (both sec and nsec from CLOCK_REALTIME)
`
  'time.now-us': `
the current Unix timestamp (microseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (microseconds)
`
  'time.parse-date': `
parses a date string into a Unix timestamp
Accepted formats: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS or YYYY-MM-DD
returns ?i64: ok = the timestamp, nil = parsing failed
s: date string (YYYY-MM-DD, or the full form with HH:MM:SS)
ts: ok=the corresponding Unix timestamp (seconds), nil=insufficient length, illegal separator/digits, or field out of range
`
  'time.parse-time': `
parses a date string into a time
Accepts the same formats as parse-date: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS or YYYY-MM-DD
returns ?time: ok = the time, nil = parsing failed
s: date string (same format as parse-date)
t: ok=the parsed time (nsec=0), nil=parse failed
`
  'time.since-ms': `
milliseconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting timestamp (milliseconds)
diff: milliseconds elapsed so far
`
  'time.since-s': `
seconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting timestamp (seconds, typically taken from now-s/os.now)
diff: seconds elapsed so far
`
  'time.since-time': `
seconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting time point
diff: seconds between now and start (nsec ignored)
`
  'time.since-us': `
