  'txt.at': `
[deprecated] txt.at: character (code point) index read — use t[idx] instead (native indexing, returns ?char)
txt.at: gets the code point value at a character position (for a byte offset use t.byte(i))
returns b ?byte: nil when out of range; a code point beyond the byte range is truncated
idx: code point (character) index
b: ok=the code point value (truncated when it exceeds the byte range), nil=out of range
`
  'txt.byte': `
returns the raw byte value at BYTE offset i (a raw byte read)
Different from txt[i]'s code point indexing; for code that builds the buffer byte by byte
Returns ?byte: nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= len-bytes()), and the caller must handle the option
Handled by the compiler as a builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts it and expands a bounds check plus GEP+load, with
no std function body; the signature is registered in src/builtin/str.go's BuiltinMethodList
(Intercepted, sharing one entry with str.byte through the bare-name fallback)
i: byte index (not a code point)
c: ok=the raw byte at that position, nil=i out of range
`
  'txt.clear': 'empties the text (len=0)'
