yes: whether it ends with sub
`
  'txt.eq': `
Compare whether two txt are equal
⚠️ Rewritten 2026-09-21. The old implementation used a single `->` pipeline (`.byte(k) - b.byte(k); c != 0 -> ok = 0;
k == an - 1 -> ok == 0 -> eq = false; k = k + 1`) only backtracks on the **last byte**,
so `t('hello').eq('world')` — "same length, different content" — always returned true
—— the `neq:` line in `tests/txt.no` kept printing 1, and it was frozen into the golden baseline.
txt.compare already compares byte by byte and branches correctly when lengths differ, so reuse it directly.
b: the target txt to compare against
eq: true=both sides byte-identical
`
  'txt.eq-full': `
exact equality of two txt values (length plus byte-by-byte)
b: comparison target txt
yes: whether the length and every byte are all equal
`
  'txt.fields': `
splits on whitespace into []txt (consecutive whitespace counts as a single separator)
out: slice of non-empty child txts split on runs of whitespace
`
  'txt.fill': `
Fill the entire data area with a byte value
val: the byte value used to fill the entire data area
`
  'txt.from-bytes': `
Create a txt from []byte (truncated to 255 bytes)
returns a txt, truncating if the []byte exceeds 255 bytes
b: the source byte
out: content copied into the txt (parts beyond 255 bytes are truncated)
`
  'txt.from-chars': `
builds a new txt from a char slice (written code point by code point, UTF-8 encoded; anything past 255 bytes is dropped)
⚠️ you cannot write code points one at a time with `val[i]: { ok -> it = cs[i] }`: D9's place-match gates on a
READ, and reading `val[0]` on a fresh txt with len=0 goes out of range and returns nil, so the ok arm never runs and
the whole function returns an empty string (txt.from-chars silently lost data because of this).
txt.new (ForwardFunc: chars-new) exists precisely for this: it writes all code points at once and sets the byte length.
cs: the code point slice ([]char)
t: ok=a new txt encoded per code point in UTF-8, nil=the encoded result exceeds 255 bytes (the excess discarded)
`
  'txt.from-hex': `
decodes hexadecimal text into a txt
returns Option: ok(txt) / nil (empty) / err('msg')
out: ok=the decoded txt, nil=empty, err=illegal hex characters or odd length
`
  'txt.from-str': `
Create a txt from str (truncated to 255 bytes)
returns a txt, truncating if the str exceeds 255 bytes
s: the source str
out: content copied into the txt (parts beyond 255 bytes are truncated)
`
  'txt.index': `
finds the position of a substring (starting from 0)
sub: the sub-txt to look for
pos: code point position of the first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'txt.index-from': `
searches for a substring starting at from
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
target: the sub-txt to look for
from: the start code point position (inclusive)
pos: code point position of the first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'txt.join': `
[]txt.join: joins a txt slice with a separator (truncating past 255 bytes)
sep txt: the separator
returns out txt: the joined result
`
  'txt.last-index': `
an alias of rindex
target: the sub-txt to look for
pos: code point position of the last occurrence, -1 when not found (alias of rindex)
`
  'txt.len': `
returns the character (code point) count (aligned with str.len; for byte length use txt.len-bytes())
n: number of code points (characters), aligned with str.len (use len-bytes() for byte length)
`
  'txt.lines': `
splits on newlines into []txt (supports \n and \r\n)
out: slice of lines split on newlines (handles \n and \r\n; a trailing newline yields no empty line)
`
  'txt.new': `
txt.new: build a txt from a char slice (module-level constructor, returns a concrete txt)
compile-time builtin (ForwardFunc: chars-new): []char is converted byte by byte into bytes with the length written once,
No byte-by-byte .no loop. txt is a builtin type whose bytes/length cannot be manipulated in user code, so the whole conversion is emitted by the compiler as IR.
anything beyond 255 bytes is dropped automatically (the length byte is clamped to 255).
cs: the code point slice ([]char)
t: the txt after byte-by-byte re-encoding (parts beyond 255 bytes dropped automatically)
`
  'txt.repeat': `
repeats the content times times (capped at 255 bytes)
returns a new txt
times: repetition count
val: a new txt after concatenating times copies (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-char': `
replaces every old-ch with new-ch (UTF-8 code points)
returns a new txt (truncated past 255 bytes)
old-ch: the code point that was replaced
new-ch: the code point after replacement
val: a new txt after per-codepoint replacement (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-n': `
replacement with a bounded count
old txt, new txt, n i64: the maximum number of replacements (n<=0 replaces nothing)
old: the child txt that was replaced
new: the child txt after replacement
n: maximum replacements (n <= 0 means no replacement)
val: a new txt after limited-count replacement (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-str': `
replaces every occurrence of the substring old with new
returns a new txt (truncated past 255 bytes)
old: the child txt that was replaced
new: the child txt after replacement
val: a new txt after replacement (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.reverse': `
Reverse the text content
out: a new txt with the bytes reversed
`
  'txt.rindex': `
reverse search for the last occurrence of a substring
returns the position, or -1 when absent
target: the sub-txt to look for
pos: code point position of the last occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'txt.rindex-from': `
reverse search for a substring starting at from (returns the last occurrence at position >= from)
target: the sub-txt to look for
from: the reverse-search lower bound (inclusive, code points)
pos: code point position of the last occurrence (>= from), -1 when not found
`
  'txt.set-byte': `
Returns ?bool (it can fail): nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= the 255-byte physical capacity), and the write is SKIPPED;
some(true) on success. The bound is the physical capacity (a txt is a fixed 255-byte buffer) rather than
len, so builders that "fill the buffer first, then set len" (fill / from-str / to-hex / join) keep working as before.
A caller may discard the ?bool as a bare statement (optunuse only covers bound names).
i: byte index
b: the byte value to write
ok: ok=true=write succeeded, nil=i out of range (write skipped)
`
  'txt.slice': `
takes the substring [start, end) and returns a new txt
start: starting code point index (inclusive)
end: the end code point index (exclusive)
out: a new txt cut by the code point range [start,end)
`
  'txt.split': `
splits on a separator into []txt
sep: the separator txt
