  'ws.listen-on': `
Create a WebSocket server and start listening
host str: IP address to bind (e.g. '0.0.0.0')
port i64: port to listen on
returns s ?server: ok = server, nil = failure
`
  'ws.next-rand': `
Produce the next pseudorandom i64
Combines a global counter with a simple LCG
r: the new pseudorandom number (global counter + LCG output, not cryptographically secure)
`
  'ws.server-conn.close': 'Close the client connection'
  'ws.server-conn.handshake': `
Complete the WebSocket HTTP handshake
Reads the client HTTP upgrade request, validates the required headers, and sends the 101 response.
Sets .connected = true on success.
`
  'ws.server-conn.is-connected': `
Check the connection state
returns yes bool: true = connected
`
  'ws.server-conn.recv': `
Blocking read of one complete WebSocket message
Supports collecting fragmented messages (continuation opcode).
Automatically answers ping with pong; on close sets connected=false and returns nil.
returns msg ?message:
ok = message
nil = connection closed
err = error
`
  'ws.server-conn.recv-nb': `
Note: to suit non-blocking polling, this method does not accumulate fragmentation across calls;
a single-frame text/binary message (the nowork frontend's usage) becomes one message directly.
Use the blocking recv when full fragment reassembly is required.
msg ?message: ok=complete message assembled by this poll; nil=no data yet or the frame has not fully arrived; err=connection closed or error
`
  'ws.server-conn.send': `
Send a WebSocket frame (server does not mask)
opcode i64: message type (WS-OP-TEXT / WS-OP-BINARY / ...)
data str: message content
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-binary': `
Send a binary message
data str: message content
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-close': `
Send a close frame
close-code i64: close status code (e.g. 1000)
reason str: close reason
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-ping': `
Send a ping
data str: ping content
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-pong': `
Send a pong
data str: pong content
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-text': `
Send a text message
data str: message content
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'ws.server.accept': `
Accept a new WebSocket client connection and complete the handshake
returns c ?server-conn: ok = handshaked client, nil = failure
`
  'ws.server.close': 'Close the WebSocket server'
  'ws.server.listen': `
Start listening
host str: IP address to bind (e.g. '0.0.0.0')
port i64: port to listen on
returns ok bool: true = success
`
  'x25519.fe-add': `
Field element addition h = f + g
h: result summed limb by limb (no carry propagation; equivalence is guaranteed by later mul/serialization)
f: left addend
g: right addend
`
  'x25519.fe-add-v': `
Return a + b
a: left addend
b: right addend
h: newly created sum field element
`
  'x25519.fe-copy': `
Copy a field element
h: destination field element (filled in place with f's 10 limbs)
f: source field element
`
  'x25519.fe-cswap': `
Conditional swap (used by the Montgomery ladder)
mask b: 0 = no swap, 1 = swap
a: first field element (participates in the swap in place)
b: second field element (participates in the swap in place)
mask: 0 = no swap, 1 = swap (expanded internally to an all-ones mask to stay constant-time)
`
  'x25519.fe-frombytes': `
Load a field element from 32 bytes (little-endian)
ref10 style: limbs are loaded unmasked via shifts, then carry propagation runs
Carry propagation uses the rounding constant (1<<(bits-1)), yielding a "half-normalized" form (limbs may be small negatives)
This form matches what fe-tobytes expects, guaranteeing a correct round-trip
h: field element after loading (10 limbs, half-normalized carries propagated)
s: 32-byte little-endian encoding (the top bit255 is masked off)
`
  'x25519.fe-frombytes-v': `
Return a field element loaded from 32 bytes (little-endian)
s: 32-byte little-endian encoding (bit255 ignored)
h: newly created field element
`
  'x25519.fe-invert': `
Compute a^(-1) mod p
Uses a ref10 addition chain to compute a^(p-2) = a^(2^255-21)
Addition chain fully aligned with libsodium fe25519_invert
out: field element to write the result into (10 limbs)
a: field element to invert (must not be zero)
`
  'x25519.fe-invert-v': `
Return a^(-1) mod p
a: field element to invert
h: newly created inverse (a^(p-2))
`
  'x25519.fe-mul': `
Field element multiplication h = f * g mod p
Uses ref10 cross-term expansion
Note: an array t[] stores intermediate results to avoid compiler optimization issues
h: product field element (f × g mod p)
f: factor (10 limbs)
g: factor (10 limbs)
`
  'x25519.fe-mul-v': `
Return a * b mod p
a: factor
b: factor
h: newly created product field element (a × b mod p)
`
  'x25519.fe-mul121665': `
Multiply a field element by the constant 121665 (= (A-2)/4, A=486662)
In the Montgomery ladder doubling formula z2 = E*(AA + a24*E), a24 must be 121665,
not 121666. 121666 makes ladder doubling wrong (all-zero/incorrect output for some scalars).
Stores intermediate results in an array to avoid compiler optimization issues
h: field element after multiplying by 121665
f: field element to multiply
`
  'x25519.fe-one': `
The one element
h: field element written as the identity (limb0=1, the rest 0)
`
  'x25519.fe-one-v': `
Return the one field element
h: newly created identity field element (length 10)
`
  'x25519.fe-sq': `
Field element squaring h = f^2 mod p
Delegates directly to fe-mul
h: square result (f² mod p)
f: field element to square
`
  'x25519.fe-sq-v': `
Return a^2 mod p
a: field element to square
h: newly created square result
`
  'x25519.fe-sub': `
Field element subtraction h = f - g
h: result subtracted limb by limb (limbs may be negative; later normalization converts them back to a positive representation)
f: minuend
g: subtrahend
`
  'x25519.fe-sub-v': `
Return a - b
a: minuend
b: subtrahend
h: newly created difference field element
`
  'x25519.fe-tobytes': `
Serialize a field element to 32 bytes (little-endian)
Carry propagation via floor division without rounding constants
Limbs are first normalized to [0, 2^bits-1], then a +19 test checks whether the value is >= p
s: 32-byte buffer receiving the serialized result
h: field element to serialize (normalized to [0,p) before output)
`
  'x25519.fe-tobytes-v': `
Serialize a field element to 32 bytes (little-endian) and return it
[32]byte is passed by value, so calling fe-tobytes(s, h) directly would not write back into the return slot;
hence the value is written to a local array first, then assigned wholesale to the return value s.
h: field element to serialize
s: newly created 32-byte little-endian encoding
`
  'x25519.fe-zero': `
The zero element
h: field element written as zero (all 10 limbs set to 0)
`
  'x25519.fe-zero-v': `
Return the zero field element
h: newly created zero field element (length 10)
`
  'x25519.x25519-gen-keypair': `
Generate a random key pair
seed i64: random seed
returns priv [32]byte: private key
returns pub [32]byte: public key
seed: rand seed (determines the private key; real scenarios must inject system entropy)
priv: private key (already clamped: bit0-2 cleared, bit254 set, bit255 cleared)
pub: corresponding public key (priv × basepoint)
`
  'x25519.x25519-get-basepoint': `
Return the basepoint u coordinate (9, 0, 0, ..., 0)
base: 32-byte little-endian encoding of the basepoint u=9
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult': `
X25519 scalar multiplication
scalar [32]byte: scalar (private key)
point [32]byte: point coordinate (public key)
returns out [32]byte: the point-multiplication result (shared secret), little-endian field-element bytes
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult-base': `
Compute scalar * basepoint
scalar [32]byte: scalar (private key)
returns out [32]byte: public key
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult-base-bytes': `
Compute scalar * basepoint ([]byte version)
scalar []byte: scalar (private key, length 32)
returns out []byte: public key (length 32)
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult-bytes': `
X25519 scalar multiplication ([]byte version)
scalar []byte: scalar (private key, length 32)
point []byte: point coordinate (public key, length 32)
returns out []byte: result (length 32)
`
